      "side": 1,
      "message": "`vma_insert_pages_slices()` advances `addr +\u003d PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL` (`16KB`) on every iteration even when the initial `addr` has a non-zero slice offset `off \u003d vma_slice_offset(vma, addr) \u003c\u003c PAGE_SHIFT_4KB`.\n\nWhen `off \u003e 0` (for example, `addr \u003d vma-\u003evm_start` with `vma-\u003evm_slice_off \u003d 3`, so `off \u003d 12KB`):\n- Iteration 0 (`pages[0]`, `addr \u003d vm_start`) maps only `PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL - off` (`4KB`, `[vm_start, vm_start + 4KB)`).\n- Iteration 1 (`pages[1]`) jumps to `addr \u003d vm_start + 16KB` instead of `vm_start + 4KB`. Inside `vma_insert_page_slices()`, `target` is set to `vm_start + 16KB` while `addr` backs up by `min(off, addr - vma-\u003evm_start)`. If the VMA is 8KB or 12KB (`vm_end \u003c vm_start + 16KB`, `vma_kernel_pages \u003d 2`), `target` lies outside `[vm_start + 4KB, vm_end)`. Consequently, `addr !\u003d target` is true for every slice of `pages[1]`, so if the PTE at `vm_start + 4KB` is already populated (`-EBUSY`), `if (ret \u003d\u003d -EBUSY \u0026\u0026 addr !\u003d target) ret \u003d 0;` silently swallows `-EBUSY` instead of returning `-EBUSY` with the remaining `*num`.\n\nAdvancing `addr` to the start of the next kernel page\u0027s slice window (`addr +\u003d PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL - off` on the first iteration, or stepping by the actual bytes mapped) keeps `target` aligned to the first slice of each page.",
